  "st-john-paul-ii.html": {
   ", poetry, and plays. By the Holy See's own count, he proclaimed 1,338 Blesseds and 482 saints, putting before the Church ordinary people as examples of holiness.": 1,
   "A quarry worker who became a priest in secret, a Polish cardinal who became the first non-Italian pope since Adrian VI in 1523, and a pilgrim who carried the Gospel to 129 countries. He opened his pontificate with three words the late twentieth century needed - \"Do not be afraid\" - and he lived them through an assassin's bullet, the fall of a dictatorship, and a long public dying. He is a canonized saint of the universal Church, and this page is his story, his teaching in his own words, and where to watch him.": 1,
   "Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith, \"The Message of Fatima\" (2000) - the assassination attempt of May 13, 1981": 1,
   "In 1942, in secret, he entered Kraków's clandestine seminary. He was ordained a priest on November 1, 1946, sent to Rome for a doctorate on faith in the works of Saint John of the Cross, and came home to parish work among students and young families - kayaking with them, hearing confessions on hikes, teaching that faith had to be argued, not inherited. He became an auxiliary bishop of Kraków in 1958, its archbishop in 1964, a cardinal in 1967, and one of the most active bishops at the Second Vatican Council. On October 16, 1978, at fifty-eight, the conclave elected him pope - the first Pole, and the first non-Italian since Adrian VI in 1523.": 1,
   "Karol Wojtyła was born on May 18, 1920, in Wadowice, a small town in southern Poland. Loss came early: his mother died when he was eight, his only brother when he was twelve, and his father - an army officer who prayed on his knees at night - when he was twenty. He studied Polish language and literature at the Jagiellonian University in Kraków until the Nazi occupation closed it in 1939. To avoid deportation he worked as a manual laborer, first in a limestone quarry, then in a chemical plant, and he acted in an underground theater that kept Polish culture alive where words could be weapons.": 1,
   "Los Angeles Times, January 16, 1995 - the Manila World Youth Day Mass, attended by about 4 million": 1,
   "No pope before him had treated the whole world as a parish. Over 104 international journeys he visited 129 countries, kissing the ground on arrival - a ritual he said honored the people of each place and the Christ already present among them. He founded World Youth Day in 1985 and drew some of the largest crowds ever gathered, including an estimated four million in Manila in 1995. He was the first pope known to enter a synagogue and the first to enter a mosque, he prayed with leaders of other Christian churches and other religions, and on the Day of Pardon in March 2000 he publicly asked God's forgiveness for the sins committed by members of the Church across history.": 1,
   "The Holy See: official biography of John Paul II - the life dates, Wadowice, the underground seminary, the 104 visits outside Italy, and the totals of 1,338 beatified and 482 canonized": 1,
   "The Holy See: statistics of the pontificate - 104 visits outside Italy, in 129 different nations": 1,
   "on April 27, 2014, by Pope Francis, alongside Pope John XXIII. His feast day is October 22, the anniversary of the Mass that inaugurated his pontificate (he was elected on October 16, 1978).": 1
